digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56657-5
  rankdir="TB";
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given hypha-associated exacerbation of intestinal inflammation and hyphal morphogenesis controlling commensalism versus invasion, therefore hyphal growth is generally associated with increased pathogenicity/inflammation and host invasion across contexts."];
  "I2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given hyphae induce higher IL-1β and IL-1β-inducing gut strains correlate with increased ulcerative colitis severity, therefore hypha-associated triggers of IL-1β are clinically relevant."];
  "I3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given an abundant hypha-specific surface protein is a plausible mediator of hypha-driven host interactions and Als3 is abundant and hypha-specific, therefore Als3 is a plausible hypha-associated mediator to study."];
  "I4" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given microbial engagement of FADD/caspase-8 DED machinery can alter epithelial death modes and IL-1β and DED machinery regulates inflammation and cell death, therefore engaging this machinery could explain inflammatory pathology."];
  "I5" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given C. albicans proteins have not been reported to directly influence intracellular death-core components and direct modulation would imply a novel intracellular recognition/modulation mechanism, therefore hypothesize an unexpected direct fungal protein interaction with intracellular death-core components could exist."];
  "I7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given hypha-associated IL-1β/colitis severity suggests clinically relevant hypha triggers and identifying hypha-associated effectors linking to IL-1β/cell-death signaling is key, therefore hypothesize a hyphal effector such as Als3 could connect hyphal growth to death/IL-1β rewiring."];
  "I8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given disease severity correlates with IL-1β output and hypha-associated triggers of IL-1β are clinically relevant, therefore mechanisms that regulate hypha-driven IL-1β are candidate drivers and therapeutic targets in ulcerative colitis."];
  "I9" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given disseminated candidiasis arises from opportunistic transition in vulnerable patients and mortality can exceed 40% even with antifungal treatment, therefore disseminated candidiasis is a severe high-mortality outcome motivating mechanistic studies and improved interventions."];
  "K1" [label="(0,0,0) If a fungal hypha-specific surface protein is abundant during hyphal growth, then it is a plausible mediator of hypha-driven host interactions relevant to inflammation/invasion."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) If a microbial factor engages (binds/modulates) key host cell-death regulators (e.g., FADD/caspase-8 DED machinery), then it can alter epithelial death modes and downstream inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-1β)."];
  "K3" [label="(0,0,0) If disease severity correlates with a specific host inflammatory output (e.g., IL-1β), then mechanisms controlling that output are candidate drivers and therapeutic targets."];
  "K4" [label="(0,0,0) If a vFLIP-like strategy inhibits/reshapes apoptosis by binding FADD/caspase-8 through DED interactions, then any non-DED protein that binds those DEDs could functionally mimic vFLIPs in modulating cell-death signaling."];
  "K5" [label="(0,0,0) If extracellular organisms generally lack direct molecular access to intracellular death-core components, then observing such direct modulation would indicate a novel intracellular recognition/modulation mechanism."];
  "K6" [label="(0,0,0) If hyphal transition is central to commensalism-versus-invasion balance and also elevates IL-1β, then identifying hypha-associated effectors that connect to IL-1β/cell-death signaling is a key research direction."];
  "N11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, Als3 is the most abundant Als-family protein and is hypha-specific."];
  "N13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, DEDs are protein–protein interaction domains (in FADD/caspase-8/cFLIP) that regulate inflammation and cell death (apoptosis, pyroptosis, necroptosis)."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, in ulcerative colitis, high-damaging C. albicans strains form hyphae and exacerbate intestinal inflammation."];
  "N20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, vFLIPs inhibit death-ligand-induced apoptosis by interacting with FADD and/or caspase-8 via DED–DED interactions."];
  "N22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, as an extracellular organism, C. albicans proteins have not been reported to directly influence intracellular core cell-death components at the molecular level."];
  "N23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, hyphal Als3 interacts with the DEDs of FADD and caspase-8, modulating immune responses and cell-death pathways."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, in immunocompromised/critically ill patients, gut-commensal C. albicans can transition to an opportunistic pathogen causing disseminated candidiasis (a leading nosocomial bloodstream infection cause)."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, disseminated candidiasis mortality can exceed 40% even with antifungal treatment."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, hyphal morphogenesis controls the balance between gut commensalism and invasive infection."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, C. albicans hyphae induce higher IL-1β secretion than the yeast form."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, gut C. albicans strains that strongly induce IL-1β in phagocytes correlate with increased ulcerative colitis severity."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate and mechanistically evaluate the hypothesis that the hypha-specific Candida albicans protein Als3 links hyphal pathogenicity to host FADD/caspase-8 DED-dependent cell-death and IL-1β-associated inflammatory responses, with relevance to ulcerative colitis and severe candidiasis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When hypha-associated Candida biology, clinically relevant IL-1β inflammation, candidate hypha-specific effectors, and host DED-mediated death-signaling mechanisms converge, they jointly motivate mechanistic investigation of how the candidate effector links fungal morphology to host immune and cell-death outcomes."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Hyphal Als3 engagement of FADD and caspase-8 DED machinery makes Als3 a plausible microbial factor capable of altering host cell-death signaling and downstream inflammatory cytokine responses such as IL-1β."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because vFLIPs inhibit death-ligand-induced apoptosis through interactions with FADD and/or caspase-8 DEDs, proteins capable of binding those DEDs are plausible candidates to test for vFLIP-like modulation of cell-death signaling."];
  "N2" -> "I1" [label="induction-case"];
  "N5" -> "I1" [label="induction-common"];
  "N7" -> "I2" [label="induction-case"];
